# SYNOPSIS

```usage
env [-i] [-u var] ... [var=value] ... [cmd [arg ...]]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`env` changes its environment as its arguments say, then runs <cmd> with the <arg>s in it, or, with no <cmd>, prints the environment, one `name=value` to a line.

: `-i`
  Start from an empty environment instead of the one `env` was given.
: `-u` <var>
  Remove <var> from the environment; it may be given more than once.

Each <var>`=`<value> after the flags sets <var>; the first argument without `=` is <cmd>.
<cmd> is looked for in the directories of `PATH` unless it holds a `/`, and is run in place of `env`, as the same process, so its exit status is the command's.

`env` is sbase's, and found only in the POSIX namespace: `/boot/bin/posix/env` is a hard link to `sbase-box`, which runs the command its name names, and `/lib/ns/posix` (a manifest's `ns=posix`, see namespace(6)) binds `/boot/bin/posix` before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program that runs `env` finds it there.
There is no native `env`.

# SEE ALSO

printenv(1), nohup(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/env.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

A <cmd> that cannot be run is reported on standard error, as `env: execvp nothing: No such file or directory`, and `env` exits 127 if it was not found and 126 otherwise.
A bad flag, or `-u` without <var>, prints the usage message and exits 1.
Printing the environment, `env` exits 1 if the output cannot be written and 0 otherwise.

# BUGS

`-` alone, POSIX's old spelling of `-i`, is taken as the name of a command.

Untested on VectraOS: running a <cmd>, and every flag.
A file that is not a program is handed by the C library to `/bin/sh`, which VectraOS does not have.
